   177	## JSON Rules
   178	
   179	- `links` are group-level legal findings. One status applies to the whole
   180	  many-to-many group.
   181	- `atomic_links` are traceability rows only. They inherit the group
   182	  `relationship`; do not invent pair-level final statuses.
   183	- `contract_ids` contain clauses that provide material coverage for the group.
   184	  Background clauses can be cited in reasoning, but should not become final
   185	  candidates unless they affect coverage or status.
   186	- `coverage_ledger` is derived from final `links`, `unmatched_matrix`, and
   187	  `unmatched_contract`, plus profile-based `out_of_scope` / `not_applicable`
   188	  matrix closures. Do not use it to invent final conclusions.
   189	- `out_of_scope` and `not_applicable` matrix closures mean the requirement is
   190	  not a risk for the current product, lot, terminal, payment method, or legal
   191	  regime. They stay in `coverage_ledger.matrix` only and do not appear in
   192	  final `unmatched_matrix`.
   193	- `unmatched_contract` contains only legally significant `extra_in_contract`
   194	  rows. Non-material rows stay in `coverage_ledger.contract`.
   195	- `relationship = aligned` requires empty `discrepancies`, `risk_level = none`,
   196	  and text-grounded confirmation that the contract package preserves the matrix
   197	  legal result.
   198	- `relationship = deviation` requires at least one discrepancy with `type`,
   199	  `description`, and `risk`.
   200	- Missing mandatory applicable requirements should normally be `high` risk.
   201	  Missing optional applicable requirements should normally be `low` or
   202	  conditional risk.
   203	- All final ids must be visible in source files. Do not use corrected,
   204	  inferred, translated, parenthetical, or internal ids.
   205	- Weak candidates do not appear in final `links`; use
   206	  `unmatched_matrix[].rejected_candidates`.
